{quote}[https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/OrderService.ts#L48|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/OrderService.ts#L48] - it seems without this, refunds won’t work{quote}

The current code is actually sufficient here, because OrderFinished is still guaranteed to happen, and using its {{created}} looks good here.

{quote}[https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/EventProcessors/PaymentOrderFinishedProcessor.ts#L119|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/EventProcessors/PaymentOrderFinishedProcessor.ts#L119] - it seems without this, the fine email won’t be sent{quote}

This is hard, but this will come up only with Partner Fines, I’ve added a TODO and description to [https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/BRE-175|https://taxify.atlassian.net/browse/BRE-175|smart-link] . 

{quote}[https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/TaskProcessors/UserInvoiceProcessor.ts#L249|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/acd6dd8fad1a169f02052f89589f7b94e4565ae4/src/rental/carsharing/rental_car_billing/main/service/TaskProcessors/UserInvoiceProcessor.ts#L249] - this seems to block a fine invoice{quote}

This has been easy to fix fortunately, because we already have {{organic event ID}}.

Having said all that, the whole flow & confusion is a bit messy, hard to follow and understand, and it would be good to clear it up.

My favorite idea is to create a zero-price Record when Order is being Finalized (i.e. in the same transaction as Invoice Email task is being created), with {{organic_event_id}}. This way, we never have to query organic events by order ID & city ID.

If not Record, we can use a separate log table.

[~accountid:641d5e830e6828ab20269e6d] has performed a data breach assessment (based on the ENISA method) which resulted in a risk rating of *Medium*. Based on this, the assessment will be obtained by the DPO (as the Global DPO is currently unavailable, the assessment will be performed by the Data Protection monitoring officer). 

{quote}*Recommendation:* In principle, the two guidelines of the EDPB consider the risk in the disclosure of first and last name under normal circumstances (without supplementary information) to be low. Also the name is common in the country (Lithuania) and therefore several persons bear this name and the person is already aware of the incident. So under normal circumstances no notification is required. 
{color:#ff5630}*However, in case of a negative completion of the CS-ticket, the user may have a possible connection between the negative completion of the CS-ticket and the disclosed name, which leads to an increased  risk to the rights and freedoms of the (external) CS-employee. If an internal investigation by our privacy team reveals a negative completion of the CS-ticket, it is recommended - based on legal caution - to report the incident to the DPA* {color}{color:#ff5630}*_(noting also that it was a human error and the (external) CS-employee was also informed immediately)_*{color}{color:#ff5630}*, as it cannot be ruled out that the there is a risk and that person whose data was disclosed and already feels scared will lodge a complaint to the supervisory authority.* {color}
If the controller decides that no notification should be made, the case must be monitored further and appropriate steps taken at the first sign of contact by the former user. {quote}

----

*Risk Level (ENISA method):* {color:#ffc400}*Medium* {color}

----

*Remarks:*
In order to examine the incident in more detail, the recommendations of the EDPB will also be addressed. The EU Network and Information Security Agency (ENISA), with the participation of the French and German DPAs, has made recommendations for a method of assessing the severity of a breach that may be useful to controllers and processors in their investigation and documentation. However, this approach of scoring using a formula based on the criteria of processing context, identification risk, and circumstances of the breach is not shared in the approach of the Article 29 Working Party or EDPB. The Article 29 Working Party/EDPB recommends that the following criteria be considered in the assessment.

* *Notification obligation:* {color:#0747a6}A notification of a data breach is required unless it is unlikely to pose a risk to the rights and freedoms of data subjects. The GDPR explains that this can include loss of control over their personal data, limitation of their rights, discrimination, identity theft or fraud, financial loss, unauthorised reversal of pseudonymisation, damage to reputation, and loss of confidentiality of personal data protected by professional secrecy. It can also include any other significant economic or social disadvantage to those individuals. A disclosure of a name (which is also commonly used) does usually not contain the risk for the above mentioned examples. Nor does the leak include any special categories of personal data or information relating to criminal convictions and offenses.{color}
* *Nature of the breach:* {color:#0747a6}Due to a human error, the first and last name of an external CS employee was shared with a user in the app. This is not a system-related error. {color}
* *Nature, sensitivity and scope of the personal data involved:* {color:#0747a6}first- and last name of an external CS employee{color}
* *Ease of identification of individuals:* {color:#0747a6}Significant{color}
* *Severity of the threatened consequences for individuals:* {color:#4c9aff} first and last name. An identification is therefore easily possible. According to the local staff (Lithuania), this is not an unusual name combination, so there is a larger number of people with this name. {color}
* *Specific characteristics of individuals:* {color:#0747a6}it does not impact a child or other vulnerable person who may be exposed to greater risk as a result. However, it is an (external) employee for whom the employer therefore has corresponding duties of care by the employee. {color}
* *Special characteristics of the data controller:* {color:#0747a6}no special characteristic of the controller (eg. Hospital, Public Authority,…){color}
* *Number of data subjects:* {color:#4c9aff}1 (one){color}
* *Additional points:* 
** *Deleted Account:* {color:#0747a6}The user's account has since been deleted and there is no reliable information as to whether the information transmitted was read by the user before the account was deleted. Also it is impossible to reach out to the user to request a deletion.{color}
** *(External) employee is already informed:* {color:#0747a6}The employee is already aware that her name first- and last name was accidentally disclosed to a user. The fact that the external CS employee is aware of the mistake can be considered as a mitigating factor.{color}
** *Common name of the (external) employee:*{color:#0747a6} According to the local team, the names are standard local first and last names, so it is easy, but not immediately possible, to identify the exact person by name. {color}
** *(External) CS - employee is scared:* {color:#0747a6}The situation is aggravated by the fact that the person is frightened by the disclosure and restricts their use of social media out of fear.{color}
* *List of examples provided by the EDPB:*{color:#0747a6} {color}
** *Ordinary circumstances:* {color:#0747a6}In the {color}[{color:#0747a6}“Guidelines 9/2022 on personal data breach notification under GDPR” Adopted 28 March 2023{color}|https://edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-04/edpb_guidelines_202209_personal_data_breach_notification_v2.0_en.pdf]{color:#0747a6} the EDPB mentions that, regarding the nature, sensitivity, and volume of personal data, the disclosure of the name and address of an individual in ordinary circumstances is unlikely to cause substantial damage. But it depends on the circumstances if there is an increased risk.{color}{color:#ff5630} In order to clarify whether a severe circumstance exists, it must be evaluated whether the handling of the CS ticket was negative for the user and whether the user is most likely to be dissatisfied with the handling of her/his concerns. {color}
** [*Guidelines 01/2021 on Examples regarding Personal Data Breach Notification*|https://edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2022-01/edpb_guidelines_012021_pdbnotification_adopted_en.pdf] _The EDPB provided Guidelines on examples for data breaches which complement the Guidelines WP 250 and it reflects the common experiences of the SAs of the EEA since the GDPR became applicable. Its aim is to help data controllers in deciding howto handle data breaches and what factors to consider during risk assessment._
*** The guidelines list examples where the risk source is an internal human error where no malicious action led to the breach.
In the example *cases 13 and 15* the EDPB is describing cases, *where names (plus additional information like e-mail addresses and food preferences) of multiple people were shared with a known group of people*, described the risks deriving from the nature, the sensitivity, the volume and the context of the personal data as low because *_“in this particular case no risk can be identified that the breach will lead to physical, material or non-material damages of the data subject due to the unauthorised disclosure”_*_, considered the breach as unlikely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of the data subjects (No notification)._ 
* *Mitigating measures:* {color:#0747a6}External CS was not contacted yet, but is avoiding checking her social media accounts, responding to the calls, after this incident. She was informed by Bolt that if someone contacts her and writes stuff, she should  escalate it to the legal team in this ticket or create a new one with the description of actions that the user took.{color}

