*Recommendation:*
The ENISA rating , calculated by the DPMO, resulted in a  (low) {color:#ffc400}*medium rating*{color} (2.23). Furthermore, as long as no feedback is received from the driver on the deletion request, it appears to be a risk of {color:#ff5630}*non-material damage (risk of being contacted by the driver that causes considerable discomfort) and a significant loss of control over personal data (Name +phone/email + information about how the person rated the service of the person who received the data)*{color}, especially since some of the riders whose data was disclosed, assuming that they did so anonymously, provided negative feedback to the driver. This is aggravated by the fact that it concerns the data of 168 people. 
*--> Notification to ICO recommended (risk for non material damage (discomfort that the contact data was disclosed to a person, the was rated by the rider) and significant loss of control over personal data because of an human error (168 cases)*

----

*Lead Authority: Bolt Operations UK OR Bolt Operations OU*

* In our privacy notice 3 controllers are listed. No Article 26 agreement dealing with DSARS in the UK: Article 26 specifies that joint controllers shall determine their respective responsibilities for compliance with the GDPR. This will include determining *which party will have responsibility for complying with the obligations under Articles 33 and 34.* (_WP29 recommends that the contractual arrangements between joint controllers include provisions that determine which controller will take the lead on, or be responsible for, compliance with the GDPR’s breach notification obligations. This is why it is paramount to have such agreements in place and with ease of access by the Privacy Legal Team and/or oDPO so that such responsibilities could be confirmed in case of a data breach”)_
* Regarding UK and EU there is no one-stop-shop-mechanism. Bolt has to check if there are Data Breach Notification Requirements in every involved country.( e.g.: The local Bolt entity in UK is processing data of data subjects from the UK without involvement of the Parent Company in Estonia (Controller outside of the European Union). The processing is not *likely* to substantially affect individuals in a EU state and outside of the EU)
* *This is a disclosure of personal data of persons in the UK to a person in the UK (one of the joint controllers is in the UK), therefore a notification must be made to the ICO in the event of a notification. → Online Form* [https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/report-a-breach/personal-data-breach/report-a-data-breach-online-form/report-a-personal-data-breach-online-form/|https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/report-a-breach/personal-data-breach/report-a-data-breach-online-form/report-a-personal-data-breach-online-form/|smart-link] 

*ENISA: SE = DPC x EI + CB*
DPC: 2 (Simple Data but increased plus 1, because it combines Phone Number, Mail, Name)
EI: 1 (Maximum)
CB: 0,25
*SCORE: 2,25*

*ICO&EDPB Guidelines:*
*EDPB:* In order to examine the incident in more detail, the recommendations of the EDPB will also be addressed. The EU Network and Information Security Agency (ENISA), with the participation of the French and German DPAs, has made recommendations for a method of assessing the severity of a breach that may be useful to controllers and processors in their investigation and documentation. However, this approach of scoring using a formula based on the criteria of processing context, identification risk, and circumstances of the breach is not shared in the approach of the Article 29 Working Party or EDPB. The Article 29 Working Party/EDPB recommends that the following criteria be considered in the assessment.

* *Notification obligation:* A notification of a data breach is required unless it is unlikely to pose a risk to the rights and freedoms of data subjects. The GDPR explains that this can include loss of control over their personal data, limitation of their rights, discrimination, identity theft or fraud, financial loss, unauthorised reversal of pseudonymisation, damage to reputation, and loss of confidentiality of personal data protected by professional secrecy. It can also include any other significant economic or social disadvantage to those individuals. A risk exists when the breach may lead to physical, material or non-material damage for the individuals whose data have been breached"
{color:#ff5630}--> The data was provided as part of a DSAR request from the driver. Art 15 (4) UK GDPR explicitly provides that the right to obtain a copy shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of others. In this case, personal data of 168 people was shared due to a human error. A disclosure of a first name together with phone number and mail address to a driver is a significant loss of control over personal data (Name +phone/email + information about how the person rated the service of the person who received the data). The scope and combination of the data passed on are likely to make someone concerned. {color}
{color:#ff5630}It is conceivable and there is a risk that the recipient of the data can use this data to contact the person whose data has been disclosed to him. This, coupled with the fact that some riders have given negative feedback on the assumption that they are doing so anonymously, can cause them considerable discomfort (non-material damage).{color}
* *Nature of the breach:* {color:#bf2600}Due to a human error, the first name, mail, phone number and other data like  Rider Rating and provided Driverrating (+ comments) of 168 riders were disclosed to one driver{color}
* *Nature, sensitivity and scope of the personal data involved:* 
{color:#ff5630}Order Id	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Order type	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Rider id	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Rider phone	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Rider email	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Rider first name	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Rider app version	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Client rating	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Client rating comment	{color}
{color:#ff5630}Payment method (no payment/financial data like card number){color}
{color:#ff5630}provided Driver rating	{color}
{color:#ff5630}provided Driver rating comment{color}	
* *Ease of identification of individuals:* {color:#bf2600}*Significant/Maximum*{color}
* *Severity of the threatened consequences for individuals:* {color:#ff5630}*TBC*{color}
* *Specific characteristics of individuals:* {color:#ff5630}*TBC*{color}
* *Special characteristics of the data controller:*{color:#ff5630} no special characteristic of the controller (eg. Hospital, Public Authority,…){color}
* *Number of data subjects:* {color:#ff5630}168{color}
* *List of examples provided by the EDPB:*
** *Ordinary circumstances:* *In the* [*“Guidelines 9/2022 on personal data breach notification under GDPR” Adopted 28 March 2023*|https://edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2023-04/edpb_guidelines_202209_personal_data_breach_notification_v2.0_en.pdf] *the EDPB mentions that, regarding the nature, sensitivity, and volume of personal data, the disclosure of the name and address of an individual in ordinary circumstances is unlikely to cause substantial damage. But it depends on the circumstances if there is an increased risk.* *In order to clarify whether a severe circumstance exists, it must be evaluated whether the handling of the CS ticket was negative for the user and whether the user is most likely to be dissatisfied with the handling of her/his concerns.*
** [*Guidelines 01/2021 on Examples regarding Personal Data Breach Notification*|https://edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2022-01/edpb_guidelines_012021_pdbnotification_adopted_en.pdf] *The EDPB provided Guidelines on examples for data breaches which complement the Guidelines WP 250 and it reflects the common experiences of the SAs of the EEA since the GDPR became applicable. Its aim is to help data controllers in deciding howto handle data breaches and what factors to consider during risk assessment.*
*** The guidelines list examples where the risk source is an internal human error where no malicious action led to the breach.
In the example cases 13 and 15 the EDPB is describing cases, where names (plus additional information like e-mail addresses and food preferences) of multiple people were shared with a known group of people, described the risks deriving from the nature, the sensitivity, the volume and the context of the personal data as low because{color:#ff5630} {color}“in this particular case no risk can be identified that the breach will lead to physical, material or non-material damages of the data subject due to the unauthorised disclosure”. But in this case , as long as no feedback is received from the driver on the deletion request, there is a risk of {color:#ff5630}*non-material damage (risk of being contacted by the driver that causes considerable discomfort) and a significant loss of control over personal data (Name +phone/email + information about how the person rated the service of the person who received the data)*{color}, especially since some of the riders whose data was disclosed, assuming that they did so anonymously, provided negative feedback to the driver. 
* *Mitigating measures:* Immediately after the controller discovered the incident, he reached out to the driver (9. November 2023) 
_”Dear Naeem Patel,_
_We are reaching out to you in relation to Case #263856445 created by you on August 31, 2023 on Bolt platform._
_There was an error in handling the personal data of another person shared in the file with you. In this case, we shared with you first name, email, phone number, and other information about passengers. We kindly request that you delete that file from all your devices if it was saved by you. Our oversight in managing this data was our responsibility, and we apologize for any inconvenience caused._
_Please remember that processing the data about the passengers in any manner by you should be in compliance with the applicable privacy regulations._
_We kindly ask you to inform us once all the data is deleted from your devices.”_
{color:#ff5630}*No feedback from the driver yet.* {color}

